      "source_id": "unreconciled:step-q183288",
      "problem": "$\\,$ $x$ is rational and $y$ is irrational. \n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is rational and $y$ is irrational\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is rational and }y\\text{ is irrational}) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is rational}∧y\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is rational})∨¬(y\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not rational}∨y\\text{ is not irrational} \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not rational or }y\\text{ is not irrational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
